a("% and INSERT after the sentence ending '... absorbs most of the modelling error.' the following two paragraphs:")
a("\\paragraph{Hardware items.} The unit price of 150~kUSD per MVA of leg rating covers the converter legs and their DC link at $H_\\mathrm{dc}=8$~ms; the coupling transformers, the 100~Hz filter (or additional DC-link capacitance) and any Scott up-rating are added as an annualised hardware term $J_\\mathrm{hw}$ (Table~\\ref{tHW}; the Scott transformer needs no up-rating at 40~MVA per leg, section~4.4). With central unit costs and CRF $=0.10185$ the two coupling transformers add " + f"{it['transformers']*0.10185:.0f}" + "~kUSD/yr at the proposed rating, the filter " + f"{(it['filter_C']+it['filter_L'])*0.10185:.0f}" + f"~kUSD/yr, and the filter loss {Sc['fixed_v7_prop']['filt']:.1f}~kUSD/yr. Table~\\ref{{tExHW}} repeats the comparison of Table~\\ref{{tEx}} with these items. The annual objective of the certified design (11.27~MVA, $\\varepsilon_u=1\\%$) is {r1(x['dJ_min'])}\\% lower than that of corrected percentile sizing at its minimum rating ({x['Jfix']:.1f} against {x['Jmin']:.1f}~kUSD/yr; lower in {pw['pct_min_J']['weeks_lower']} of 60 weeks, paired difference $-{abs(pw['pct_min_J']['mean']):.1f}\\pm{pw['pct_min_J']['ci95']:.1f}$~kUSD/yr) and {r1(x['dJ_opt'])}\\% lower than with the rating optimised ($C={x['Copt']:.2f}$~MVA, {x['Jopt']:.1f}~kUSD/yr). Capital, hardware and losses are {lh(x['dCLs_min'])} and {lh(x['dCLs_opt'])}, and the exceedance {r0(x['dEv_min'])}\\% and {r0(x['dEv_opt'])}\\% lower; the first figure repeats the finding of Table~\\ref{{tEx}} that the advantage over percentile sizing at its minimum rating is almost entirely exceedance. Re-selecting the rating under the new costs gives {x['Cresel']:.2f}~MVA with the same threshold; keeping 11.27~MVA costs {x['pen']:.1f}~kUSD/yr more, so the design verified in sections~4.4--4.7 is kept. Over the low--high unit costs of Table~\\ref{{tHW}} and the two filter alternatives (filter or $H_\\mathrm{{dc}}=26.2$~ms) the objective is {r0(xr['exact_dJ_min'][0])}--{r0(xr['exact_dJ_min'][1])}\\% lower than for the percentile design at its minimum rating and {r0(xr['exact_dJ_opt'][0])}--{r0(xr['exact_dJ_opt'][1])}\\% lower than for the optimised one (Table~\\ref{{tHWsens}}); it remains {r0(N['exact']['TFS']['dJ_min'])}\\% lower than the percentile design even if the Scott transformer had to be up-rated to 50~MVA per leg.")
a("")
a(f"\\paragraph{{Capital plus losses at equal exceedance.}} The percentile family leaves the exceedance in the remaining 5\\% of the time unconstrained, so capital and losses are compared at the same exceedance. For a cap on $E_\\mathrm{{viol}}$ equal to that of the percentile design with optimised rating ({x['cap1']:.1f}~\\%$\\cdot$h/yr) the cheapest percentile design ($\\varepsilon_u=1.9\\%$, $C$ free, weekly criterion in at least 95\\% of the weeks) needs $C={eq_a['pct']['interp']['C']:.2f}$~MVA and {eq_a['pct']['interp']['caploss']:.0f}~kUSD/yr of capital, hardware and losses; the cheapest proposed design ($C$ and $\\varepsilon_u$ free) needs $C={eq_a['prop']['interp']['C']:.2f}$~MVA ($\\varepsilon_u={eq_a['prop']['interp']['eps']:.2f}\\%$) and {eq_a['prop']['interp']['caploss']:.0f}~kUSD/yr, i.e.\\ {r0(x['eq1']['kUSD'])}~kUSD/yr ({r1(x['eq1']['pct'])}\\%) less (Table~\\ref{{tEq}}, Fig.~\\ref{{fEq}}). With the exceedance of the proposed design as cap ({x['cap2']:.1f}~\\%$\\cdot$h/yr) the percentile family needs {eq_b['pct']['interp']['C']:.2f}~MVA and the saving is {r0(x['eq2']['kUSD'])}~kUSD/yr ({r1(x['eq2']['pct'])}\\%). Between evaluated candidates the exceedance is interpolated in $C$ on a logarithmic scale; eleven additional candidates evaluated with the exact model on all 60 weeks agree with this interpolation within 0.3\\% in $E_\\mathrm{{viol}}$ and 0.03~kUSD/yr in the loss cost. Over the unit-cost cases the advantage is {rg(min(xr['exact_eq1_pct'][0],xr['exact_eq2_pct'][0]),max(xr['exact_eq1_pct'][1],xr['exact_eq2_pct'][1]))}\\%; with approximation (8) it is {rg(N['approx']['TF']['eq1']['pct'],N['approx']['TF']['eq2']['pct'])}\\% (60 weeks) and for the suburban line {rg(N['sub']['TF']['eq1']['pct'],N['sub']['TF']['eq2']['pct'])}\\% (40 weeks, approximation (8)), where at the same $w_3$ the capital plus losses were {lh(S['scenarios']['TF']['fixed_v7_prop_delta_vs_pct_min']['dcaploss_pct'])} than for percentile sizing at its minimum rating.")
